  "summary": "El Cerrito's 40 Flock Safety cameras will be removed between July 30 and Aug. 18, 2026, after the City Council voted in May to end its three-year $315,000 contract with the company. Police confirmed cameras have been offline since June 6, with red lights being motion-activated infrared signals rather than active recording. The article notes prior federal-agency access to El Cerrito ALPR data in 2023 and contrasts the decision with actions in Richmond, Oakland, Mountain View and other Bay Area cities.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Amid concerns Flock Safety cameras in El Cerrito are still functioning despite officials agreeing to end a contract with the surveillance company, the police department said the cameras are not operating and will soon be coming down.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Crews will begin removing the 40 Flock Safety cameras installed in the city between July 30 and Aug. 18",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "Multiple federal agencies had access to El Cerrito's license plate reader data between June 2023, when the cameras were first installed, and August 2023",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
    }
  ],
